.TH TANGSIBLE-REVISIT 1 "August 2026" "Tangsible" "Tangsible Manual"
.SH NAME
tangsible-revisit \- browse and reopen previous runs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tangsible revisit
.RI [ "playbook.yml or role name" ]
.RI [ "\-\-tags | \-t"
.IR tags ]
.RI [ "\-\-limit | \-l"
.IR hosts ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B revisit
lists every past invocation that still has its raw event data saved
under
.B .tangsible/runs/
(see FILES in
.BR tangsible (1)),
newest first. Selecting one replays its saved
.B .jsonl
event stream into a fresh tree \(em the exact same live tree UI
.BR tangsible-run (1)
uses, already frozen with that run's own final outcome, tags, and
hosts.
.PP
With no arguments, every recorded run across every playbook and role is
shown. An optional
.I playbook.yml
or
.I role
argument narrows the list to just that target;
.B \-\-tags
/
.B \-t
and
.B \-\-limit
/
.B \-l
narrow it further, to runs whose own recorded tags/hosts overlap the
given value.
.PP
Once a run is open, every normal live\-tree key applies (see
.BR tangsible-run (1)),
including
.B r
to rerun it (starting a real, live invocation from that point) and
.B d
to diff it against another recorded run. The one difference: pressing
.B Esc
at the bare tree level (not inside a dialog) closes that entry and
returns to the
.B revisit
list, rather than quitting the program \(em only
.B q
/
.B Ctrl\-C
at the list itself does that. If a rerun is started from within a
revisited entry and then interrupted or finishes,
.B q
/
.B Ctrl\-C
there still only returns to the list, never exits outright.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The list was shown (and browsed, however briefly) and closed normally
with
.B q
/
.BR Ctrl\-C .
.TP
.B 1
No recorded run matched the given filters, and the list was never shown
at all.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tangsible (1),
.BR tangsible-run (1),
.BR tangsible-rerun (1)
